.TH MV 1
.SH NAME
mv  \-  move or rename files and directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mv
file1 file2
.PP
.B mv
dir1 dir2
.PP
.B mv
file ... directory
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mv
moves (changes the name of)
.I file1
to
.IR file2 .
.PP
If
.I file2
already exists,
it is removed before
.I file1
is moved.
If
.I file2
has a mode which forbids writing,
.I mv
prints the mode (see
.IR chmod (2))
and
reads the standard input to obtain a line;
if the line begins with
.B y,
the move takes place;
if not,
.I mv
exits.
The question is asked only when the standard input is a terminal.
.PP
.B Note:
the second SYNOPSIS line is an addition.  The v7 page showed two forms and the
program has always had three \- its own usage message says
.B "mv f1 f2; or mv d1 d2; or mv f1 ... fn d1"
\- and the third of them, renaming a
.BR directory ,
is the whole of what makes
.I mv
interesting on this machine.  See below.
.PP
In the second form,
one or more
.I files
are moved to the
.I directory
with their original
file-names.
.PP
.I Mv
refuses to move a file onto itself.
.SH "RENAMING A DIRECTORY"
.B Note:
this section is new; v7's page did not mention the case at all.
.PP
There is no
.I rename
system call on this system, and no
.I mkdir
or
.I rmdir
either.  A directory is renamed by hand.  When the old and the new name share a
parent that is a
.IR link (2)
and an
.IR unlink (2)
and no more, because `..' already points where it should.  When they do
.B not
share a parent it takes four calls \- link the new name, unlink the old, unlink
the directory's own `..', and link the new parent in its place \- and until all
four have run the filesystem is inconsistent.
.I Mv
ignores every signal across that sequence for exactly that reason.
.PP
.B "Note: /bin/mv is set-user-id root, and has to be."
Both
.IR link (2)
and
.IR unlink (2)
refuse a directory to anyone but the super\-user
.RB ( "if ((ip->i_mode & IFMT) == IFDIR && !suser())" ,
kernel/sys2.c and kernel/sys4.c), so none of the calls above can be made by an
ordinary user.  The bit is `mode 04755' in root.manifest, and
.IR mkdir (1)
and
.IR rmdir (1)
carry it for the same kind of reason.
.PP
.B Root is borrowed and not granted.
.I Mv
calls
.B setuid(getuid())
as soon as it knows the argument is not a directory, so every other form of
.I mv
runs with the invoking user's own identity and no privilege whatever.  For the
directory form the privilege is kept, and what decides whether the rename is
allowed is
.IR mv 's
own
.IR access (2)
checks on both parents and on the directory itself \-
.IR access (2)
asking about the
.B real
user id.  So the answer is the one the user would have got without the setuid
bit; only the mechanics need root.
.PP
Two renames are refused outright:
.PP
A directory may not be moved
.B into itself
or into anything below it.
.I Mv
walks from the target's parent up to the root through `..' and stops if it meets
the source.
.PP
A path that spells
.B ..
in any component is refused, rather than reasoned about.  (This test is reached
only when the two parents differ; a rename inside one parent takes the simpler
branch and never looks.)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B Note:
v7's page had no DIAGNOSTICS section.  All of these go to the standard error and
.I mv
exits 1.
.PP
.BI "mv: cannot access " file
\- the source could not be stat'ed.
.PP
.B mv: directory rename only
\- a directory appeared among the arguments of a multi\-file move.  Renaming a
directory takes exactly two arguments.
.PP
.BI "mv: " file " is a directory"
.PP
.BI "mv: " file " and " file " are identical"
.PP
.BI "mv: cannot unlink " file
.PP
.BI "mv: " dir / name : " name too long"
and
.B mv: target name too long
\- a constructed name would not fit 100 characters.  v7 checked one of the three
places this can happen and did not bound the other two.
.PP
.BI "mv: " dir " exists"
\- the target of a directory rename is in the way.
.PP
.BI "mv: cannot rename " name
\- the last component of the source is `.', `..' or empty.
.PP
.B mv: cannot locate parent
.PP
.BI "mv: no write access to " dir
\- and this is the check that asks about the
.B real
user id; see above.
.PP
.B mv: cannot move directories across devices
.PP
.B mv: cannot move a directory into itself
.PP
.B "mv: Sorry, path names including .. aren't allowed"
.PP
.BI "mv: cannot link " a " to " b
and
.BI "mv: " file ": cannot unlink"
\- one of the four calls of a re\-parent failed.
.I Mv
undoes what it can and says which call it was.
.PP
.B mv: try again
\- the
.IR fork (2)
for a cross\-device copy failed.
.PP
.B mv: cannot exec cp
and
.B mv: cannot wait for cp
\- see BUGS.
.SH BUGS
If
.I file1
and
.I file2
lie on different file systems,
.I mv
must copy the file and delete the original.
In this case the owner name becomes
that of the copying process and any
linking relationship with other files
is lost.
.PP
.B Note:
`must copy the file' is literal \-
.I mv
.IR fork s
and
.IR exec s
.BR /bin/cp ,
by that name and with no search of any kind, so the move fails outright if
.I cp
is not on the filesystem.  It is not as bad as the paragraph above suggests,
though: the times
.B are
kept, because
.I mv
restores them with
.IR utime (2)
after the copy returns.  Only the owner and the links are lost.
.PP
.B "Note: this cannot happen on this machine."
A cross\-device move is one whose
.IR link (2)
came back
.BR EXDEV ,
and there is only one filesystem here \- one EC\-5052 drive is the whole of the
store, and swap lives on the drums.  The path is faithfully ported and
.B /bin/cp
really is on the image, but nothing exercises it and nothing can until there is a
second thing to mount.
.PP
.I Mv
should take
.B \-f
flag, like
.I rm,
to suppress the question
if the target exists and is not writable.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
cp(1), ln(1), rm(1), chmod(2), link(2), unlink(2), utime(2)
